theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115772/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f2818fc-787e-5b46-8a7a-c5fbea7905a6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115772"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72231/selection/efd6158d3b5200fbe2fe8be1afc1f05c8481b4117c2ff84fa04df46e74d7cf47" :answer/id #uuid "614e3443-28d3-545a-bf14-0c76137e518f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72231/selection/38c0686a321428ab8356ebb4d7fa2bb19ed503d8b484c9be4f872e5b24de53cd" :answer/id #uuid "b29dd502-29c0-5dc2-9d60-36afc147b0b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/va